net/utils: never block in net_bufpool_timedalloc from interrupt context

net_bufpool_timedalloc() takes the blocking net_sem_timedwait_uninterruptible()
path whenever timeout != 0. That is a latent bug: the TCP write-buffer, ARP and
"send receive-window update" paths call it with a non-zero timeout, and those
paths can be reached from interrupt/exception context — where a blocking sem
wait triggers an illegal context switch.

oveRTOS's Linux personality exposes this on the STM32F746: guest socket syscalls
are dispatched inside the SVCall handler (IPSR=11). A guest poll()/send() there
descends psock_poll -> tcp_should_send_recvwindow -> tcp_send -> arp_send ->
net_bufpool_timedalloc(); when the pool is momentarily empty the timed wait
blocks, NuttX attempts to switch away from the nested exception, and the resume
note hook is handed a garbage tcb -> BusFault (BFAR = tcb+offsetof(pid) = 0xa8)
that escalates to a forced HardFault. It is intermittent because it only fires
when the buffer pool is contended (concurrent RX on the HP work queue).

Blocking in interrupt context is never valid, so gate the wait on
up_interrupt_context(): fall back to the non-blocking nxsem_trywait() there. The
alloc then returns NULL, the socket op reports -EAGAIN, and the personality's
coordinator retries the op in thread context, where the timed wait is legal.
Thread-context callers are unaffected.

--- a/net/utils/net_bufpool.c
+++ b/net/utils/net_bufpool.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 #include <nuttx/kmalloc.h>
 #include <nuttx/net/net.h>
 #include <nuttx/semaphore.h>
+#include <nuttx/irq.h>

 #include "utils/utils.h"

@@ -107,7 +108,7 @@
       DEBUGASSERT(pool->nodesize > 0);
     }

-  if (timeout == 0)
+  if (timeout == 0 || up_interrupt_context())
     {
       ret = nxsem_trywait(&pool->sem);
     }
